= Ecology =

== Overview ==
'''Ecology''' is the scientific study of interactions between living organisms and their physical and biological environments. It investigates the distribution, abundance, activity and interactions of organisms, often using field observations, experiments and mathematical or statistical models.

== Scales of inquiry ==
At the '''organismal''' scale, ecology examines how an organism responds to environmental conditions. '''Population''' ecology examines the numbers and dynamics of organisms of a particular species in an area. '''Community''' ecology studies how populations of different species interact. '''Ecosystem''' ecology adds the movement of energy and matter among organisms and their abiotic environment.

These scales overlap. A process at one scale may influence another, so a study must define the spatial and temporal bounds that make its conclusions meaningful.

== Biotic and abiotic processes ==
'''Biotic''' components include living organisms and their interactions, such as competition, predation, mutualism and decomposition. '''Abiotic''' components include water, temperature, minerals, light, salinity and physical disturbance. Relationships can change over time and need not be reducible to a single factor.

== Modeling and inference ==
A field correlation between species abundance and temperature may reflect a causal effect, but it can also depend on habitat, migration, detectability, competing species or shared drivers. Experiments, long-term observation, replication and appropriate statistical models are used to compare hypotheses. A population count without a sampling model does not establish an exact population size.

== Ecosystems and global change ==
[[Related to::Ecosystem|Ecosystems]] combine organisms and abiotic conditions. Their flows include [[Related to::Carbon cycle|carbon cycling]] and energy transfer through food webs. [[Related to::Climate change|Climate change]] can alter habitats and biological responses; local consequences depend on species, location, disturbance history and interacting environmental drivers.

== Boundaries and use ==
Ecology is a natural science, not a synonym for environmental activism or an automatic endorsement of a particular management policy. Conservation or policy decisions may rely on ecological evidence but also involve societal priorities, uncertainties and trade-offs.

== Biological information versus computational ecology ==
Ecology can be studied computationally without declaring every ecosystem an information processor. For example, an investigator can represent observed counts, movement, interaction networks or resource use as data; an algorithm can estimate population dynamics or compare alternative hypotheses. These are '''models used by researchers'''. Their reliability depends on observation design, sampling bias, numerical assumptions, independent tests and uncertainty.

An '''organism''' may also have a specific information-processing mechanism: environmental sensing, molecular or neural signaling, internal-state-dependent behavior or regulatory feedback. Such mechanisms must be identified and tested at their own biological scale. Inferring a behavior from a population-level correlation does not establish how the individual organism encodes and responds to a signal.

BITwiki should distinguish (1) a measured ecological process, (2) the model or algorithm analyzing it, and (3) an evidenced information-processing mechanism inside interacting organisms. These three objects are not automatically equivalent; the independent canonical scope requires a demonstrated informational or methodological question. See [[Scientific model]], [[Measurement]], [[Feedback]] and [[BITwiki:Computational scope]].

